.TH ICONV 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
iconv \- convert text between character encodings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iconv
.RB [ \-c ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR outfile ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR fromcode ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR tocode ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B iconv \-l
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iconv
reads text in one character encoding, converts it to another, and writes
the result.
It is the busybox-w32 applet derived from win-iconv, and it does the work
with the Windows API, so the encodings it knows are the code pages that
Windows provides.
Each character is converted to UTF\-16 and then to the output encoding.
.PP
With no
.IR file ,
or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
.B iconv
reads standard input.
It writes standard output, or with
.B \-o
a file.
Operands are converted in order, each on its own, so the byte order mark
and any other conversion state start afresh with each.
But every operand other than
.B \-
opens the first operand and not itself, so give a single
.IR file ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
The input encoding
.I fromcode
and the output encoding
.I tocode
default to the ANSI code page, which is UTF\-8 on Windows 10 version 1903
and later.
The locale plays no part in the conversion.
.SS Encoding names
A name is one of these, tried in this order:
.IP \(bu 2
Empty, or
.BR char :
the ANSI code page.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR wchar_t :
UTF\-16 little endian, with no byte order mark.
.IP \(bu 2
A decimal Windows code page number, optionally preceded by
.B CP
or
.B XX
in any case, as in
.BR CP1252 ,
.BR 437 ,
or
.BR cp65001 .
Any text after the digits is ignored, so
.B CP850x
means 850.
The code page must be one that Windows reports as valid.
.IP \(bu 2
An alias from the list that
.B \-l
prints, such as
.BR UTF\-8 ,
.BR UTF\-16LE ,
.BR ASCII ,
.BR ISO\-8859\-1 ,
.BR WINDOWS\-1252 ,
.BR SJIS ,
.BR EUC\-JP ,
.BR GB18030 ,
.BR BIG5 ,
or
.BR KOI8\-R .
Case does not matter.
.PP
The names
.B char
and
.B wchar_t
must be lowercase.
Since the number test comes before the list, aliases in the list that
begin with
.B CP
are read as numbers, and five of them fail:
.BR CP367 ,
.BR CP819 ,
and
.B cp1025
mean code pages 367, 819, and 1025 instead of 20127, 1252, and 21025,
and
.B CP\-IS
and
.B CP\-GR
mean code page 0.
A name that does not resolve, or whose code page Windows lacks, makes
.B iconv
print
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
iconv: iconv_open error: Invalid argument
.EE
.RE
.PP
and exit.
.SS Name suffixes
A name may end in suffixes that begin with
.BR // ,
such as
.BR ASCII//TRANSLIT .
They may be combined and are matched without regard to case.
An unknown suffix is ignored.
Only suffixes on
.I tocode
matter, except as noted for
.BR //NOCOMPAT .
.TP
.B //TRANSLIT
Convert a character that has no equivalent in the code page to its
nearest equivalent by the system's best-fit table, such as an accented
letter to its base letter, or else to the code page's default
character, usually
.BR ? ,
instead of failing.
It has no effect on the UTF encodings, ISO\-2022\-JP, or EUC\-JP.
.TP
.B //IGNORE
Discard what cannot be converted, as
.B \-c
does.
.TP
.B //NOCOMPAT
Turn off a compatibility mapping.
By default, when the output is code page 932 (SJIS), 51932 (EUC\-JP), or
50220 to 50222 (ISO\-2022\-JP), nine characters are first replaced by
their CP932 equivalents:
U+00A5 becomes U+005C, U+203E becomes U+007E, U+2014 becomes U+2015,
U+301C becomes U+FF5E, U+2016 becomes U+2225, U+2212 becomes U+FF0D, and
U+00A2, U+00A3, and U+00AC become U+FFE0, U+FFE1, and U+FFE2.
Code page 20932 has its own table, which also applies to its input,
so this is the one suffix that matters on
.IR fromcode .
.SS Byte order marks
.BR UTF\-16 ,
.BR UTF16 ,
.BR UCS\-2 ,
.BR UCS2 ,
and
.B UCS\-2\-INTERNAL
mean UTF\-16 little endian with a byte order mark (BOM).
On input, a leading BOM of
.B FF FE
or
.B FE FF
is removed and sets the byte order, which is little endian without one.
On output,
.B FF FE
is written before the first character, so empty input gives empty
output.
.BR UTF\-32 ,
.BR UTF32 ,
.BR UCS\-4 ,
and
.B UCS4
do the same with 32-bit units and the BOM
.BR "FF FE 00 00" .
.PP
Names that give a byte order, such as
.BR UTF\-16LE ,
.BR UTF\-16BE ,
.BR UTF\-32LE ,
and
.BR UTF\-32BE ,
neither write nor remove a BOM, so a BOM in the input becomes the
character U+FEFF.
.B UTF\-8
neither writes nor removes one.
.SS Errors
Input is rejected if it is invalid for
.IR fromcode ,
such as an unpaired UTF\-16 surrogate, a UTF\-32 value above 10FFFF, or a
byte above 7F in ASCII.
Output is rejected if
.I tocode
cannot represent a character.
On the first such failure
.B iconv
writes what it has converted so far, prints
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
iconv: conversion error: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
on standard error, and exits with status 1.
The message gives no position.
.I reason
is
.B Illegal byte sequence
for the cases above, and
.B Invalid argument
when the input ends inside a character or is invalid GB18030.
.B \-c
and
.B //IGNORE
turn these failures into discards.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-cfUTF\-8 ,
and an option's argument may be attached or be the next argument.
There are no long options.
Options may follow operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Of an option given more than once, the last counts.
.TP
.B \-l
Print the name of each encoding the program knows, one per line, and
exit, ignoring any other options and operands.
The names are in the order of the program's table and not sorted, and
some appear more than once.
The list has neither the numeric forms nor
.B char
and
.BR wchar_t ,
and it includes names that do not work: the five
.B CP
names described above, the code pages that convert nothing (see
.BR NOTES ),
and code pages that not every system has, such as
.B CP154
and
.BR CP1133 .
.TP
.B \-c
Discard characters that
.I tocode
cannot represent, and bytes that are invalid in
.IR fromcode ,
without a message.
An invalid sequence is discarded one byte at a time.
This is the same as adding
.B //IGNORE
to
.IR tocode .
.TP
.BI "\-o " outfile
Write to
.I outfile
instead of standard output.
The output goes to a temporary file in the same directory, named
.I outfile
with six characters appended, which replaces
.I outfile
when every operand has been converted, even if
.I outfile
is read-only.
So
.I outfile
may also be an input.
.TP
.BI "\-f " fromcode
Read text in the encoding
.IR fromcode .
The default is the ANSI code page.
.TP
.BI "\-t " tocode
Write text in the encoding
.IR tocode .
The default is the ANSI code page.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit with status 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first operand ends the options, so that later arguments
such as
.B \-t
are file names.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when
.B iconv
starts, even in quoted operands, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is.
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or
.B \-l
or
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or a missing option argument, an unknown encoding, a
file that could not be opened or replaced, or a conversion error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert a Windows\-1252 file to UTF\-8:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ iconv \-f CP1252 \-t UTF\-8 old.txt >new.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert a file in place:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ iconv \-f LATIN1 \-t UTF\-8 \-o names.txt names.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert UTF\-16 text, with or without a BOM, to UTF\-8:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ iconv \-f UTF\-16 \-t UTF\-8 log.txt >log\-utf8.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write UTF\-16 with a BOM, for programs that expect one:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ iconv \-f UTF\-8 \-t UTF\-16 notes.txt >notes\-utf16.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Discard the characters that ASCII cannot hold:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ iconv \-c \-f UTF\-8 \-t ASCII names.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Approximate them instead:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqcaf\e303\e251\en\(aq | iconv \-f UTF\-8 \-t ASCII//TRANSLIT
cafe
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the ISO 8859 encodings:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ iconv \-l | grep \-i 8859
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU iconv:
.IP \(bu 2
The encodings are Windows code pages, not libiconv's converters.
ISO\-2022\-JP and EUC\-JP work, through MLang.
Other code pages whose characters can exceed two bytes, apart from
UTF\-8 and GB18030, are accepted but convert nothing: ISO\-2022\-KR
(50225), HZ\-GB\-2312 (52936), UTF\-7 (65000, which has no alias), the
ISCII pages (57002 to 57011), and others.
Without
.BR \-c ,
output in them fails at the first character, and input fails or hangs
as described below.
With
.BR \-c ,
everything is discarded.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options, no
.BR \-s ,
and no
.BR \-\-verbose .
.IP \(bu 2
The defaults for
.B \-f
and
.B \-t
are the ANSI code page, not the locale's.
.IP \(bu 2
.B //TRANSLIT
is Windows' best-fit mapping, not libiconv's transliteration.
.IP \(bu 2
Conversion errors give no position.
.PP
Only the first operand can be a file.
An operand other than
.B \-
is never opened by its own name: the first operand is opened instead.
So
.B iconv a b
converts
.I a
twice and never opens
.IR b ,
and
.B iconv \- a
looks for a file named
.BR \- .
Run
.B iconv
once for each file.
.PP
Input is converted in blocks of 512 bytes.
Normally a character that straddles a block boundary is carried over to
the next block, but with
.B \-c
or
.B //IGNORE
it is not: the part at the end of the block is discarded as invalid, and
so is the rest of the character at the start of the next block.
So
.B \-c
corrupts any multibyte character that falls across a boundary.
.PP
Without
.BR \-c ,
some input makes
.B iconv
loop forever instead of failing with
.BR "Invalid argument" :
input of 512 bytes or more in a code page that converts nothing, and
GB18030 input with an invalid byte sequence followed by 511 or more
bytes.
.PP
With
.BR \-o ,
the temporary file stays behind after any failure, including a bad
encoding name or an input that cannot be opened, and
.I outfile
is left as it was.
If
.I outfile
is a directory, the command fails with
.BR "Is a directory" .
.PP
Standard input, standard output, and files are read and written in
binary mode, so CR LF line endings and Ctrl-Z characters are ordinary
characters to convert.
.B iconv
does not change line endings.
Convert UTF\-16 text to an ASCII-compatible encoding before changing its
line endings with
.BR dos2unix (1).
.PP
File names may use drive letters and backslashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
A directory operand fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
.PP
When standard output is a console, the output is treated as text in the
ANSI code page and converted for display, so only output in that
encoding is readable there.
Each block of up to 512 output bytes is also cut short at its first NUL
byte, as found in UTF\-16 output.
Write to a file or a pipe for any other encoding.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR dos2unix (1),
.BR od (1),
.BR xxd (1)
